some remarks to you we have presented to  President Clinton this morning the final  report of our work which details our  experiences in four and a half years of  hunting for searching locating reviewing  and releasing to the public the records  of the tragic day in Dallas of November  22nd 1963 is a voluminous file and it is  there with remarkably few redactions and  available for the American public now to  review in its entirety it we have had an  extraordinary experience as the first  group of outsiders who have had the  authority to order declassification of  government records particularly  intelligence and law enforcement records  and the president gratefully received  our report we were very happy with the  support he gave us throughout this  entire effort and it is an effort  unprecedented in American history to  have Outsiders reviewing significant and  sensitive government records and  ordering release of virtually all of  these materials  I'm Anna Nelson I'm a member of the  board and I teach history and American  University it was a great interest to me  to be on the board because I'm someone  who because of my field of study is used  to breed information a great deal and it  was very interesting to to work for once  to get documents open I think what we've  done is really enriched the historical  understanding of the period from 1961  264 when the Kennedy era basically was  in the  in the country's political center and  we've gone far beyond the assassination  itself in order to put it in some sort  of a historical context it's a very  valuable collection I want to emphasize  the fact that they have four civilians  five civilians really who were able to  declassify documents the agencies could  not stop us from looking and  declassifying if they did not agree with  us they had to come to the White House  focus the mind to have to do that why we  really were a unique body and had some  very unique experiences and we hope that  we've have prevented people in the  government that it's not dangerous to  allow records 30 35 years old to be in  the public view  I'm Kermit hall I'm dean of the college  of humanities at The Ohio State  University I really would like to send  two messages to you the first message is  that the president warmly embraced our  report spoke enthusiastically about the  need for openness in government  confirmed the wisdom of appointing the  board in the first place and underscored  the necessity of committing the United  States government to a long-term policy  of opening records to the American  people and the second is to remind all  of you that while the board is going  away today its work continues the echo  of our efforts will be heard literally  for the next decade or longer but it  also depends since some of these  documents have been postponed in their  release until the year 2017 in some  cases earlier it depends on all of us to  keep the people who are in the agencies  feet to the fire so that when those  dates come for documents to be open that  they don't shirk their legal and  constitutional responsibilities and  really the moral responsibility that's  entailed in our act actions and making  sure that the American public has the  opportunity to come to terms with the  issue of whether or not its own  government participated in the murder of  the President of the United States back  in back in the early 1960s so I would  urge all of you as well as the as time  goes by here to remember that the legacy  we present to you is the legacy of  openness and accountability in  government and it needs to be sustained  over time no matter how difficult  circumstances may become  my name is Bill Joyce I'm an associate  university librarian in the princeton  university library today is our last day  after four and a half years of effort to  form a research collection at the  National Archives that is still growing  and has now attained the size of over  four million pages this material not  only sheds light on the assassination  but on the entire period that is to say  the context of the Cold War is  illuminated in unprecedented detail as  is the inner working of the government  in a very tumultuous period in our  history this collection will be years in  the mining by researchers and it will  take a very long time to augment  understanding and to develop new more  informed interpretations of what the  importance of the period and the events  that occurred during it another part of  our work and the legacy one of the more  important of our legacies is the  precedent of what we have done is five  outside Pat board members charged for  the first time in American history to  release records of the federal  government that were previously  restricted we have developed procedures  and policies that might be applied to  other important issues other  controversies that have arisen in terms  of government behavior and we hope that  the policies and procedures that we have  developed might be applied and adapted  whenever necessary to help razoo resolve  these controversies and at the same time  to increase openness in government and  as Kermit hoja said to make government  officials as accountable as possible and  it's up to us to keep the pressure on  now that the board has gone out of  business to see to it that the  obligations that have been undertaken  and the promises to release records are  fulfilled thank you  any questions for us this morning yes go  ahead  it's the right  we have to remember that this event  occurred during the height of the Cold  War and for that reason all records  virtually on this event were sealed all  the CIA's records were sealed the FBI  records were sealed many for for many  many decades into the future there was a  penchant for secrecy a desire to close  everything off in the name of national  security when the board reviewed these  records and applied its balanced  judgment we found little reason to  continue to protect these 