nt effort to secure records  from Belarus and also from the former  Soviet Union now Moscow we know jack and  I and our former executive director went  to Belarus we went to Minsk we met with  the KGB we saw the records they have is  about a stack of about five feet we  weren't able to get those records out  our relationship with the Belarusian  govern  the moment is not the best and hasn't  been the best but more fundamentally  those materials we believe are really  essential to understanding Oswald and  his his life really two years in in  mince was intensively followed under  surveillance the most personal intimate  details of his life were contained in  those records and we would hope over  time that the Belarusian government and  our state Department of National  Archives are somebody can work out a  deal to get copies of those in the  National Archives we also know and we  feel very strongly there are additional  materials that are held in moscow and we  would hope again that through some  efforts we would be able to get those  materials and into our work as well we  didn't have quite I think the level of  success we would have liked to have had  and working within the apartment of  state but I know that over time that  they'll give an even greater effort to  to helping us in that regard one of the  one of the problems that arose with the  investigations answer some questions  there is that each time a group  investigated Warren Commission the House  Select Committee on the there was a  Rockefeller Commission the church study  the Frank church is committing all of  these investigations then took their  records and hid them put them away close  the door on them so that in addition to  the general suspicion of the agencies  was a general suspicion of all the  investigations and these needed to be  released and that's something else I  fact our statute required that we look  at each and every one of them and  release them for the most part we have  we obviously protect the names of people  who  might be in jeopardy in various small  things like that but I think that was a  very important part of the suspicion the  fact that nobody ever investigated and  then let anybody ever see the records  they used to investigate are you saying  the Warren Commission did you have and  have all the information no the Warren  thorne Commission had only 18 months and  they took what was given to them they  took the records of CI he brought them  and they took the records the FBI  brought them and they concluded they  were asked to conclude very quickly they  were asked to conclude so that the  country could settle down there was a  lot of we know now from records in the  Lyndon Johnson library in other places  that there was a great concern that this  might there might be some element of  inclusion than part of the Communists  for example so the idea was to quickly  get it over with and they moved very  quickly in 18 months wasn't quite enough  time to go out and do their own kind of  Investigation  I think the next step I think we'd all  agree is for people to use that  collection it is very very well if  there's a database it's the first  collection the National Archives it has  such a wonderful database so it can be  searched it can be searched on the net  and if people will use the collection  and we'll start putting all the pieces  together conspirators generally don't  write it down but you can create a  mosaic you can create from these records  hopefully as Kermit Hall said a sense of  what really happened rather than  speculation of what might have happened  and and I think we all agree that we  hope that some of the records that we  have noted should be released in five  years some in 10 years should be  monitored to make sure that them out and  that people should look very closely to  keep that mentality of openness around  I think I just like to add that I think  it's important to record that the board  concluded its work we reviewed the  records that we were able to identify we  survey the agencies very carefully we  were able to review all of the records  of the various government investigations  so from that standpoint I think we can  say that our work has been concluded in  that respect however there are still  likelihood that they may be yet  additional records that may have been  misfiled they may have been misplaced  they may have been misunderstood there  are in addition to that records that  were redacted that for which the  redactions ought to come off sometime  between now and the year twenty  seventeen when all of the records are  released and we want to know that there  is an advocate now that we are going out  of business that there can be continuing  attention to make sure that that  information is released as it's  scheduled to be I think with just a  quick follow-up what will what will go  on now is the extensive work of going  through these files by individuals who  are qualified to review them carefully  and make conclusions from them I think  that our legacy hopefully will also be  that through this you new and unique  method of reviewing records and ordering  release that maybe we will taught some  of these government agencies that it is  better to be open than secret in many  cases and that perhaps some more trust  can be restored to a public that is is  weary of lies that government made  in the past so that one of the key  findings of your committee was that  there needs to be more openness in  government and you said the president  endorsed that today do you see any irony  the fact that you're meeting with the  president took place in secret well no I  don't see any irony in that you know the  president graciously made some time  available on our schedule because he  wanted to hear about our report I know  he's got a busy schedule today and what  we have tried to do as much of our work  and open as possible we've conveyed to  you really everything that was said by  